{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take every change to agreed requirement text, including a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement, through the same review and agreement before committing it.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to take every change to agreed requirement text through the same review and agreement before committing it. That covers rewordings for precision and removals of requirements.\n\nThe single test, `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]`, checks this in three ways:\n- **Description:** `description(\"habeas-requirements\")` must contain \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\". This puts requirement-text edits within the skill's trigger.\n- **Review step:** `step_of(..., \"Sort the new and changed requirements into groups\")` must equal 3. This pins step 3 as the review step. `step_of` is built on `workflow_steps`, which parses the numbered Workflow steps.\n- **Rule sentence:** `assert_phrases` must find the sentence \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed\". Its helper `collapse` normalizes line wrapping, so the match works on the wrapped text in the skill. I checked the `Workflow` section of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. It contains that sentence, step 3 is the review, step 4 is the user agreement gate, and step 5 is the commit. So \"steps 3 to 5\" does mean review, agreement and commit.\n\nThe test would fail if the sentence were dropped or reworded to omit the rewording or removal cases. It would also fail if the review step moved without the reference being updated, or if the description stopped covering requirement-text changes. The assertions match the requirement's wording.\n\nTwo weaknesses are minor and not gaps. `assert_phrases` searches the `SKILL.md` and its reference files together rather than `SKILL.md` alone. The test also does not assert the contents of steps 4 and 5. The sentence itself ties the obligation to those steps, and the text asks nothing further. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
